// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row method_arity_mismatch_says_no_such_method (tier 4, diag).
// An ordinary inherent method called with the WRONG NUMBER of arguments. The
// verdict is right — this must be refused — but the SENTENCE is false: `R` DOES
// have a method `thing`; it takes one argument and got none. rustc says
// "this method takes 1 argument but 0 arguments were supplied" (E0061) and
// points at the argument list.
//
// FOUND 2026-09-09 while pinning the fail half of the G156-5b pair, where the
// same sentence appears for `r.drop()` and reads as "the type has no
// destructor". MEASURED on the pre-round binary (32c88e8fa76e0a9f) and the
// post-round one: IDENTICAL. Nothing about Drop is involved — the control here
// uses an ordinary name, which is what makes it a general diagnostics defect and
// not a member of the drop-identity class.
package method_arity_mismatch_says_no_such_method;
struct R { id: i32 }
impl R {
    fn thing(self: &mut Self, k: i32) -> i32 { return k; }
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut r: R = R { id: 1i32 };
    let n: i32 = r.thing();
    return n;
}
